WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.084
You have one hello-devops stack, on port 8080.

2
00:00:06.084 --> 00:00:08.879
Now you want a second one for testing.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.240
Copying the folder works for a week.

4
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:15.557
Then someone fixes a setting in one copy, and forgets the other.

5
00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:17.795
Now every fix has to be made twice.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.745
A module is a folder of .tf files.

7
00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:25.163
You write the shape of the stack once, in a folder called hello-stack.

8
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:29.301
Every name inside it is built from an input, var.name.

9
00:00:29.301 --> 00:00:30.393
So is the port.

10
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.930
The folder where you run terraform is the root module.

11
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.459
It calls hello-stack with a module block.

12
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:39.831
And it can call it again, with a different name and port.

13
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.868
It works like a function.

14
00:00:41.868 --> 00:00:44.291
The variables are its parameters.

15
00:00:44.291 --> 00:00:48.181
The call dev passes in the name hello, and port 8080.

16
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.771
The resources are the side effects.

17
00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:54.835
Out come real objects: hello-net, hello-redis and hello-web.

18
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.907
Call it twice, and you get two sets of real objects.

19
00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:02.525
The test call makes hello-test-web, on port 8082.

20
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:03.388
But wait.

21
00:01:03.388 --> 00:01:06.955
Docker refuses two containers with the same name.

22
00:01:06.955 --> 00:01:08.556
Why is there no clash?

23
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:12.280
Because the module builds every name from var.name.

24
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.967
Hello for dev, and hello-test for test.

25
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.498
The outputs are its return values.

26
00:01:18.498 --> 00:01:21.736
The root reads them as module.dev.url.

27
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:24.419
Here is the difference from a function.

28
00:01:24.419 --> 00:01:26.934
A function call is gone once it returns.

29
00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:29.197
A module call lives on in the state.

30
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:35.992
Its resources get addresses with a module prefix, such as module.dev.docker_container.app.

31
00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:38.629
They stay until you remove the call.

32
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:40.448
Now the fix from before.

33
00:01:40.448 --> 00:01:42.601
You make it once, in the module.

34
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:46.598
And every stack that calls it changes on its next apply.

35
00:01:46.598 --> 00:01:48.009
Nothing drifts apart.

36
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:53.036
Dev, test and prod need the same shape, with small differences.

37
00:01:53.036 --> 00:01:55.901
One clean way is one folder per environment.

38
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:57.815
Each one is a short root.

39
00:01:57.815 --> 00:02:00.911
A provider, and one call to the shared module.

40
00:02:00.911 --> 00:02:02.660
Only the arguments differ.

41
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:05.252
Each folder has its own state file.

42
00:02:05.252 --> 00:02:07.979
So a prod apply has no way to touch dev.

43
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.225
Compare a workspace.

44
00:02:10.225 --> 00:02:13.579
In envs/dev, run terraform workspace new qa.

45
00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:16.933
Terraform switches to a second, empty state.

46
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.180
An empty state means nothing exists yet.

47
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:26.960
So the plan wants to create everything again, with the same name site-dev, and port 8201.

48
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:28.550
Dev already uses both.

49
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.516
Applying would fail, because the code does not use the workspace name anywhere.

50
00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:38.007
If you use workspaces, build names from terraform.workspace.

51
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.353
But workspaces share one backend.

52
00:02:40.353 --> 00:02:46.262
HashiCorp's docs say that makes them unsuitable when environments need separate credentials.

53
00:02:46.262 --> 00:02:49.731
So most teams use a folder per long-lived environment.

54
00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:53.345
A module is a folder you call with inputs.

55
00:02:53.345 --> 00:02:58.251
Each call makes its own real objects, and hands values back through outputs.

56
00:02:58.251 --> 00:03:02.835
And each long-lived environment gets its own folder, and its own state.
